#!/usr/bin/env bash
# monorepo-settings-check.sh [ROOT] -- the monorepo root's settings gate. Exit 0 clean, 1 findings
# (one `file:line: RULE message` per finding), 2 cannot run (no PyYAML, no python3).
# PYTHON=<interpreter> overrides python3. No network, no pnpm. "Unit has target T" is make's exit status for
# `make -C <unit> -q <target>`, nothing it prints. The inspection asks make about a unit in a fixed order, by EXIT STATUS only (nothing printed is read), and every question after the first carries -o <each makefile make read> (--assume-old: no makefile is remade by it, so a makefile being remade can never be the thing that answers). 1) The list pass, always first: make -C <unit> -q -f <unit makefile> -f <list makefile> <random name>; the module-owned list makefile, written into a private temp dir, records $(MAKEFILE_LIST) in a file by a parse-time $(shell printf ...) and declares .PHONY: <random control name> with no rule; the exit status of the pass is not used for the verdict (it only picks the words of the finding when no list was written), and a list that is empty, or names something that does not exist as a path from the unit directory (a name with a space or a newline splits into words that do not), leaves the unit unanswerable: error, fail closed (a name that does exist is accepted, for example ../common.mk, which is also what -o names). The list file is written atomically (a name made in the private directory, then moved over the final name), so a pass that is cut at any moment leaves a complete list or none: the pass waits for make to exit; a pass that reaches its 5 s limit is killed like any other question (SIGKILL to its process group) and, when it had written its complete list, is not an error: that list is used. A pass that wrote no list is told apart in the finding: it timed out (the finding says it timed out listing the unit's makefiles, with the seconds), make could not read the Makefile (it exited non-zero, as with an error guard or a syntax error: the finding says so and gives the exit status), or the list names something that is not a path from the unit directory (the words above). A makefile that rewrites an included makefile on every parse (version.mk: FORCE with a recipe that writes it) makes make 4.3 restart during the list pass: the pass then runs to its 5 s limit and the complete list it already wrote is used, so the unit is judged correctly but costs about 5 s per inspection there (3.81: about 0.1 s). A generated makefile whose generation takes longer than the list pass's limit is cut at the limit and the unit is then an error or judged without it: measured on 3.81 and 4.3 with a generation of sleep 8 written through a redirect, the first inspection judges the unit without it (no rule for the target) after 5.1 s, the killed generation leaves the file EMPTY (0 bytes: the redirect created it and the kill skips make's delete-on-interrupt), the next inspections say the same in 0.1 s, and the user's own make verify fails (no rule to make target) until that file is deleted or regenerated; a generation that ends inside the limit leaves a complete file (2 s: present, one generation, under the list pass only). The list pass is the one place where an included makefile that has a rule may be remade (a parse-time $(shell ...) runs there too). 2) The control question: make -C <unit> -q -f <unit makefile> -f <list makefile> -o <each listed makefile> <control name>; it answers 0 only when every makefile make read has settled; non-zero or a timeout (a makefile that make still wants to remake and cannot pin: an included makefile that is missing and whose rule does not create it, or a makefile rule that never converges) is state error, fail closed: the unit's makefiles do not settle. 3) make -C <unit> -q -o ... <target>; only if that answers 0 does it ask make -C <unit> -q -B -o ... <target> (the same question with every file considered out of date, so the verdict does not depend on whether the unit's outputs are fresh); only if the last question asked answered 2 does it ask make -C <unit> -n -o ... <target>. The random-name probe for a catch-all (a %: rule or .DEFAULT:) is asked as question 3 too, with -o. Verdict: -q 1 = present; -q 0 and -q -B 1 = present (the target was only up to date), unless a file or directory named like the target sits beside the Makefile and the rule is not .PHONY (the real run says up to date: idle, with a .PHONY hint); -q -B 0 = idle (nothing to run even with everything out of date); an answer of 2 asks -n: 0 = present, 2 = missing. A question that times out is state error (the finding says: timed out, with the limit that applied to that question), the list pass included, except a list pass that timed out after writing its complete list, which is used, and once one question for a unit timed out nothing more is asked for that unit in this run: a unit costs at most 5 s for the list pass plus one timeout (5 s for the control, 20 s for another question). Under -q make executes the recipe lines marked + or containing $(MAKE) of the target and of its prerequisites, each recipe at most up to its first line that is neither (an upper bound: it stops at the first out-of-date prerequisite whose recipe starts with a plain line; a file target that a + line just created may be deleted again). The -B question executes the same classes of lines, passed on to sub-makes in question mode as well, but MORE of them, since nothing counts as up to date. Under -n make walks the WHOLE recipe, so every + / $(MAKE) line of the target and of all its out-of-date prerequisites runs (a sub-make in dry-run mode runs its + lines and not its plain lines). Apart from the remake in the list pass and from -n, no plain recipe line runs under any question. The judgment is the same on GNU make 3.81 and 4.3 for every shape measured, and the same before and after a real run (an included makefile that has a rule, with no rule that runs anything for the target, is idle, and so is verify: gen.mk with gen.mk such an included makefile; the same unit with verify: build or a $(MAKE) build line is present; an included makefile that a rule creates, or a generated makefile that carries the target, is judged like a plain one), except: a recipe made only of + / $(MAKE) lines that run nothing (verify: with +true, +@echo ok, or $(MAKE) check-fast with check-fast: ;): 3.81 answers 1 to -q (present), 4.3 answers 0 to -q and to -q -B (idle, fail-closed: such a recipe verifies nothing); verify: +false differs too (3.81: -q 2 and -n 2 = missing; 4.3: -q 1 = present) and on both the root make verify ends non-zero; an included makefile that is missing and whose rule does not create it (-include gen.mk with gen.mk: ; @true) is error on 3.81 for every target (the control does not settle) and, on 4.3, judged by the target alone (idle without a recipe, present with one). What runs during the inspection was measured on GNU make 3.81 and 4.3.
# (the units are the repository's own trusted code).
set -uo pipefail
here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
root="${1:-$(cd "$here/.." && pwd)}"
py="${PYTHON:-python3}"
# Layout guard: this module is monorepo-only (its manifest cannot say so), so a root without the pair is no monorepo.
if [[ ! -f "$root/pnpm-workspace.yaml" || ! -f "$root/repo.yaml" ]]; then
  echo "FAIL monorepo-settings-check: $root has no pnpm-workspace.yaml + repo.yaml pair; this module is for layout monorepo only"
  exit 1
fi
exec "$py" -B "$here/monorepo_check.py" check "$root"
